From 7b1606c9928213cb88299137876601bd53966968 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zak Stowaway <hello@stowawaybar.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 16:11:18 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] Restatements: say out loud when a reported number changes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Zak: 'if my reported numbers are going to be changing, i need to know what's
happened.' Marilyna's is the case that prompted it. July, as reported on each
weekly Xero pull:

  overheads  $1,313 -> $5,663  (+331%, 8 revisions)
  Uber fees  $2,130 -> $8,529  (+300%, 6 revisions)

Every figure was right on the day; late bills kept landing against a month that
looked closed. July profit fell by thousands afterwards and nothing said so. And
it is not just Mari: 85 of 180 series have been restated, Stowaway's July
overheads by +$12,472 across 8 revisions.

Two parts, per the plan:

1. data/restatements.json — an APPEND-ONLY ledger. Each run compares the live Xero
   feeds to the last recorded value and appends a revision when it moved, so it
   needs no git history at runtime (a shallow CI checkout is fine) and the ledger
   IS the history. Seeded from the commits that already existed.
2. A 'Restatements' panel on Daily Reporting: first said -> now, the change, how
   many revisions, and a status word.

Status is a judgement and says so: provisional (still moving), settling (last
change small), final (nothing new for a fortnight). Measured off DAYS SINCE IT
LAST MOVED, not consecutive revisions — an append-on-change ledger cannot
otherwise tell 'moved last week' from 'settled in May', and my first cut had every
closed month reading provisional forever.

Deliberately NOT freezing published figures: bills arrive late, the numbers should
get more accurate. The fix is disclosure, not a locked snapshot.

Gate: 652 pytest, 11 node suites (new one included), arch_guard, schema_guard,
build_site 1204 files every reference resolves.
